---
import Headshot from "../components/Headshot.astro"
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout title="About">
  <header class="intro">
    <h1 class="label">// about</h1>
    <Headshot size={160} class="about-headshot" />
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Hello! I'm Christian Danielsen, a web developer working remotely in
      beautiful Oregon.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some things I'm into: JavaScript/TypeScript, the art of writing tests,
      continuous everything, and working on teams with a diverse group of
      people.
    </p>
    <p>
      Please feel free to{" "}
      <a href="mailto:ckdanielsen@gmail.com?subject=Ahoy, Christian!">
        get in touch!
      </a>
    </p>

    <h2>(In)Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <h3>Q: HotMess?</h3>
    <p>
      A: I hiked the <a href="https://www.pcta.org">Pacific Crest Trail</a> in
      2010. On a long trail, it's a tradition to adopt an alter ego (a trail
      name). My propensity for misplacing things landed me the title of
      'HotMess'.
    </p>
    <h3>Q: What's with the masthead?</h3>
    <p>
      A: If you work with JavaScript, you've probably encountered the famous{" "}
      <a href="https://www.destroyallsoftware.com/talks/wat">wat talk</a>,
      detailing some of JavaScript's best... eccentricities. I'm so enamored
      with the masthead expression (which evaluates to true in JS), that I'm
      considering getting a tattoo of it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin: 56px 0 32px;
  }
  .intro .label {
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .intro :global(.about-headshot) {
    display: block;
    width: 160px;
    height: 160px;
    object-fit: cover;
  }
  .prose h3 {
    margin-top: 32px;
    font-size: 19px;
  }
</style>
